# gitleaks suppressions.
#
# This repository deliberately carries key material that is not secret:
# LUKS test passphrases in tests/manifest.json, the synthetic keys
# generated by scripts/create-luks-*, and fixture UUIDs. Every one of
# them exists so the crypto read paths can be tested, and none of them
# guards anything.
#
# To suppress a finding, add its fingerprint on its own line below with
# a comment saying why it is safe. gitleaks prints the fingerprint in
# its report; the format is:
#
#   <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# or, for a finding with no commit (a working-tree scan):
#
#   <path>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# Prefer fixing a real leak over suppressing it. If a credential ever
# does land here, rotate it first -- the history scan will keep finding
# it, and suppressing the finding does not un-publish the secret.
#
# No suppressions are needed today. This file exists so that the first
# false positive can be handled with a one-line commit rather than an
# emergency workflow edit while every PR is red.
